(window.GROUP_QUIZZES=window.GROUP_QUIZZES||{})["physical-diagnosis-1-exam-3-breast-exam-quiz-version-2"]={"title":"Breast Examination — Set 2 (Physical Diagnosis 1)","category":"Physical Diagnosis","sem":"summer-1-2026","questions":[{"q":"What supplies the medial quadrant of the breast vascularly?","choices":["Perforating branches (2,3,4) from the internal thoracic artery and branches of the anterior intercostal artery","The external mammary branch arising directly from the lateral thoracic artery laterally","Pectoral branches originating directly from the thoracoacromial arterial trunk superiorly","The axillary artery directly, with no other clinically significant named branches"],"answer":0,"exp":"The medial quadrant is supplied by perforating branches of the internal thoracic artery and anterior intercostal artery branches. — Physical Examination of the Breasts lecture, Slide 10"},{"q":"What is the fibrous tissue of the breast responsible for?","choices":["Providing the primary blood supply to the tissue","Providing supportive structure for the glandular tissue","Producing milk for the infant during lactation","Draining lymphatic fluid from the glandular tissue"],"answer":1,"exp":"Fibrous tissue, including Cooper's ligament, supports the glandular tissue of the breast. — Physical Examination of the Breasts lecture, Slide 6"},{"q":"Where does adipose tissue predominate in the breast?","choices":["Both superficially and deeply, surrounding the glandular tissue","Only directly beneath the nipple and areola","Adipose tissue is not a significant component of breast composition","Only in the deepest layer, adjacent to the chest wall"],"answer":0,"exp":"Adipose tissue surrounds the glandular tissue and predominates both superficially and deeply. — Physical Examination of the Breasts lecture, Slide 6"},{"q":"Where is the nipple and areola located relative to the breast?","choices":["At the most superior edge of the breast, near the clavicle","Location varies randomly with no consistent anatomic position","At the most medial edge of the breast, near the sternum","At the lateral midline of the breast"],"answer":3,"exp":"The nipple and areola sit at the lateral midline of the breast. — Physical Examination of the Breasts lecture, Slide 4"},{"q":"What forms at the areola where each glandular duct opens, and what is its function during lactation?","choices":["A sebaceous gland that secretes an oily lubricant onto the areola","A dilated duct opening that accumulates milk during lactation","A cluster of lymph nodes draining the axillary lymphatics","A fibrous attachment point anchoring Cooper's ligaments to the dermis"],"answer":1,"exp":"At the areola, each duct dilates where it opens, accumulating milk during lactation. — Physical Examination of the Breasts lecture, Slide 4"},{"q":"Where are the subscapular (posterior) axillary nodes located?","choices":["At the lower border of pectoralis major, inside the axillary fold","Along the lateral border of the scapula, deep in the posterior axillary fold","High in the apex of the axilla, close to the axillary vein","Along the upper part of the humerus, near the axillary vein"],"answer":1,"exp":"Subscapular (posterior) nodes are located along the lateral scapular border, deep in the posterior axillary fold. — Physical Examination of the Breasts lecture, Slide 12"},{"q":"What do the infra- and supraclavicular nodes receive drainage from?","choices":["The inguinal nodes","The contralateral breast exclusively","Directly from the nipple only, bypassing all other node groups","The central nodes"],"answer":3,"exp":"Infra- and supraclavicular nodes receive drainage from the central axillary nodes. — Physical Examination of the Breasts lecture, Slide 13"},{"q":"Besides drainage toward the axilla, where else does breast lymphatic drainage go?","choices":["Only to the cervical nodes","Also to the collateral (contralateral) side","Only to the inguinal nodes","Exclusively to the axilla, with no other pathway"],"answer":1,"exp":"Breast lymphatic drainage also goes to the collateral side, in addition to the axilla. — Physical Examination of the Breasts lecture, Slide 12"},{"q":"Where are the lateral axillary nodes located?","choices":["High in the axilla, centrally","At the lower border of pectoralis major","Along the lateral border of the scapula","Along the upper humerus"],"answer":3,"exp":"The lateral nodes are located along the upper humerus. — Physical Examination of the Breasts lecture, Slide 13"},{"q":"Do some lymphatic vessels cross the midline of the chest?","choices":["Yes, some cross the midline and drain into the contralateral breast and chest wall","Yes, but only transiently during pregnancy and active lactation","No, lymphatic drainage remains strictly confined to the ipsilateral axilla","Yes, but only in patients with advanced, ulcerating breast cancer"],"answer":0,"exp":"The lecture specifically notes that some lymphatic vessels cross the midline to drain into the contralateral breast and chest wall. — Physical Examination of the Breasts lecture, Slide 14"},{"q":"How many quadrants is the breast divided into for surface anatomy description?","choices":["4 quadrants","8 quadrants","2 quadrants","6 quadrants"],"answer":0,"exp":"The breast is divided into 4 quadrants for surface anatomy description. — Physical Examination of the Breasts lecture, Slide 20"},{"q":"Is nipple inversion always abnormal?","choices":["Yes, inversion always requires biopsy","Yes, inversion always indicates malignancy","No, inverted is a normal variant","Nipple position has no clinical significance at all"],"answer":2,"exp":"Nipple inversion can be a normal anatomic variant, whereas the nipple is usually everted. — Physical Examination of the Breasts lecture, Slide 18"},{"q":"According to Tanner staging of breast development, what characterizes Stage 4?","choices":["Areola and nipple project to form a secondary mound above the breast","Breast bud stage with a round mound and increased areola diameter","Preadolescent stage, with elevation of only the nipple and no breast bud","Further enlargement with areola and breast still confluent"],"answer":0,"exp":"Stage 4 is characterized by the areola and nipple projecting to form a secondary mound above the level of the breast. — Physical Examination of the Breasts lecture, Slide 23"},{"q":"What happens to the breast during the aging process?","choices":["Breasts diminish in size, glandular tissue atrophies and is replaced by adipose tissue, and ducts become easily palpable as firm strands","Breasts remain unchanged throughout life, with no atrophy of glandular tissue or ductal changes at any age","Breasts enlarge due to glandular hyperplasia and increased vascularity, similar to changes seen in pregnancy","Nodularity increases progressively while adipose tissue is replaced entirely by dense fibrous connective tissue"],"answer":0,"exp":"Aging causes breast size reduction, glandular atrophy replaced by adipose tissue, and palpable firm ductal strands. — Physical Examination of the Breasts lecture, Slide 26"},{"q":"According to Tanner staging of breast development, what characterizes Stage 3?","choices":["Areola and nipple projecting forward to form a distinct secondary mound above the breast","Further enlargement of breast elevation, with the areola and breast still confluent","Projection of the nipple only, with the areola receded to the breast contour","Elevation of only the nipple as a small papilla, with no breast bud development yet"],"answer":1,"exp":"Stage 3 shows further enlargement of the breast with the areola and breast contour remaining confluent (not yet forming a separate mound). — Physical Examination of the Breasts lecture, Slide 23"},{"q":"During the sitting inspection, what maneuvers help exaggerate abnormal findings like dimpling?","choices":["Lying supine with both arms relaxed at the sides","Raising hands overhead and pressing hard against the hips","Holding the breath deeply for a count of thirty seconds","Bending forward at the waist, but only for small breasts"],"answer":1,"exp":"These dynamic maneuvers during sitting inspection exaggerate dimpling or retraction that might not be visible at rest. — Physical Examination of the Breasts lecture, Slide 28"},{"q":"For patients with large or pendulous breasts, what additional inspection maneuver is recommended?","choices":["Crossing the arms over the chest","Leaning forward","Lying prone","No modification is needed regardless of breast size"],"answer":1,"exp":"Leaning forward is specifically recommended for inspection of large or pendulous breasts. — Physical Examination of the Breasts lecture, Slide 28"},{"q":"What is the purpose of pressing the hands firmly against the hips during breast inspection?","choices":["It is one of the dynamic maneuvers (along with raising arms overhead) used to exaggerate dimpling/retraction and assess for skin tethering","It is used to specifically check for axillary lymphadenopathy by compressing the nodes firmly against the chest wall","It has no specific clinical purpose and is simply a comfortable resting position during the inspection phase","It relaxes the pectoral muscles for easier palpation, similar to resting the arm at the side during a supine exam"],"answer":0,"exp":"Pressing hands against the hips contracts the pectoral muscles, helping reveal dimpling, retraction, or skin tethering not seen at rest. — Physical Examination of the Breasts lecture, Slide 44"},{"q":"When examining axillary lymph nodes with the patient's arms raised, what is being assessed about the pendulous breast?","choices":["The patient must lie supine for this step to let the breast fall flat","Nothing different is done regardless of breast size, shape, or degree of ptosis","The exam is done leading the breast forward if pendulous, for better access","The exam is skipped entirely in patients with pendulous breasts"],"answer":2,"exp":"Leading the breast forward when pendulous improves access to properly examine the axillary nodes. — Physical Examination of the Breasts lecture, Slide 28"},{"q":"What general awareness should the examiner keep in mind throughout the breast exam?","choices":["Sensitivity to the patient's feelings only matters if she explicitly reports discomfort or pain","Only physical positioning and draping matter, not the patient's emotional comfort during the exam","No particular sensitivity is needed, since the exam involves no specialized equipment or instruments","Be alert to the female patient's feelings, given the sensitive nature of the exam"],"answer":3,"exp":"The lecture specifically notes the examiner should be sensitive to the patient's feelings throughout this exam. — Physical Examination of the Breasts lecture, Slide 27"},{"q":"What is assessed regarding breast size and symmetry on inspection?","choices":["Perfect symmetry between the breasts is expected in every healthy, unaffected patient","Only size is formally assessed on inspection, since symmetry is highly variable","Size and symmetry are not part of the standard inspection, only palpation findings","Size and symmetry, noting that some differences are common and normal"],"answer":3,"exp":"Size/symmetry are assessed, and the lecture notes that some asymmetry between breasts is common and normal. — Physical Examination of the Breasts lecture, Slide 29"},{"q":"What position is used for static (non-dynamic) breast inspection, and with the arms in what position?","choices":["Supine, with arms overhead","Standing, with arms crossed over the chest","Sitting, with arms relaxed at the sides","Prone, with arms behind the back"],"answer":2,"exp":"Static inspection begins with the patient sitting, arms relaxed at the sides, before proceeding to dynamic maneuvers. — Physical Examination of the Breasts lecture, Slide 43"},{"q":"What nipple findings should be specifically inspected for, including characterizing any discharge?","choices":["Only the nipple's color and pigmentation, without assessment of discharge or texture","Only whether the nipple is everted or inverted, without noting scaling or discharge","Nipple assessment is not part of the standard breast inspection and is deferred to a specialist","Inversion, retraction, scaling, or ulceration, and characterization of any spontaneous discharge"],"answer":3,"exp":"These are the specific nipple assessment findings, including noting and characterizing any spontaneous discharge. — Physical Examination of the Breasts lecture, Slide 43"},{"q":"What general appearance findings are assessed on breast inspection, besides color and nipple changes?","choices":["Contour, masses, dimpling, and retraction","Only the pattern of axillary hair growth","Only the symmetry of the underlying rib cage","Only hair distribution on the chest wall"],"answer":0,"exp":"These are additional general appearance findings assessed on breast inspection, alongside color, thickening, size, and symmetry. — Physical Examination of the Breasts lecture, Slide 29"},{"q":"What breast/axilla inspection component precedes assessment of specific findings like color and contour?","choices":["Nipple assessment always comes before general appearance","Only the axilla, never the breast itself","General appearance of the breast and axilla","Palpation always precedes inspection"],"answer":2,"exp":"Overall appearance of the breast and axilla is inspected first, before assessing specific findings like color, thickening, size/symmetry, and contour. — Physical Examination of the Breasts lecture, Slide 29"},{"q":"What mobility characteristics should be assessed for a breast mass, and what accompanying finding is noted?","choices":["Only mobility relative to the chest wall, since skin mobility is not a reliable clinical indicator","Mobility in relation to the skin and to the chest wall, noting any accompanying dimpling","Mobility is not a relevant characteristic for breast masses; only size and consistency are assessed","Only whether the mass is tender to palpation, without noting mobility characteristics"],"answer":1,"exp":"Mass mobility relative to both the skin and the chest wall is assessed, along with noting any accompanying dimpling. — Physical Examination of the Breasts lecture, Slide 34"},{"q":"What abnormal breast mass characteristics should be described, regarding shape?","choices":["Only whether the mass has visible pulsation","Only whether the mass is round or square","Round, disc-like, regular, or irregular","Shape is not assessed for breast masses"],"answer":2,"exp":"Shape descriptors for an abnormal breast mass include round, disc-like, regular, or irregular. — Physical Examination of the Breasts lecture, Slide 35"},{"q":"What consistency descriptors are used to characterize an abnormal breast finding?","choices":["Only wet or dry","Only smooth or rough","Consistency cannot be reliably assessed on palpation","Soft, hard, or firm"],"answer":3,"exp":"Consistency of an abnormal breast finding is described using the terms soft, hard, or firm. — Physical Examination of the Breasts lecture, Slide 35"},{"q":"During the axillary lymph node examination, what characteristics are assessed for each palpable node?","choices":["Only node location, not its physical characteristics","Only whether a node is present or absent","Only node color, visualized through the skin","Size, consistency, mobility, and tenderness"],"answer":3,"exp":"These are the specific characteristics assessed for axillary lymph nodes on palpation. — Physical Examination of the Breasts lecture, Slide 45"},{"q":"What abnormal breast finding characteristics should be described regarding location and size, in addition to shape and consistency?","choices":["Location and size","Only the patient's age at discovery","Only whether the finding is bilateral","Only the finding's relationship to the menstrual cycle"],"answer":0,"exp":"Location and size, along with shape and consistency, are the characteristics used to describe an abnormal breast finding. — Physical Examination of the Breasts lecture, Slide 35"}]};
